<?xml version="1.0" standalone="yes"?>

<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN [
<!ENTITY name      "godwit">
<!ENTITY author    "kmbrimble">
<!ENTITY version   "0.6.3">
<!ENTITY launch    "Settings/Godwit">
<!ENTITY pluginURL "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kmbrimble/unraid-godwit/main/godwit.plg">
<!ENTITY gitURL    "https://github.com/kmbrimble/unraid-godwit/releases/download/v&version;">
<!ENTITY plgPATH   "/boot/config/plugins/&name;">
<!ENTITY emhttp    "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;">
<!ENTITY md5       "ddf5620cf21f167198237ac51164b808">
]>

<PLUGIN name="&name;" author="&author;" version="&version;" launch="&launch;" pluginURL="&pluginURL;" support="https://github.com/kmbrimble/unraid-godwit" min="7.0" icon="upload">

<CHANGES>
###0.6.3
- Fixed the folder-picker (Browse) losing edits. Done was at the very bottom
  of the scrolling list while the only visible control, the titlebar close,
  threw the whole selection away with no warning. Done and Cancel now sit in a
  button bar pinned below the list, and closing any other way (titlebar close,
  Esc) asks first if you have unsaved changes. The expand arrows are now drawn
  with CSS, so they no longer show as blank boxes on browsers lacking the glyph.
###0.6.2
- Fixed Google's own daily upload limit showing as a red "error". Overnight
  2026-09-21 the Photos job uploaded 220.7 GB cleanly, then Google stopped it
  with "User rate limit exceeded" and Godwit stored it as a generic error.
  That stop is Google's per-account quota (on Google's clock, separate from
  Godwit's own 700 GiB budget) and is now recognised, shown as "paused —
  Google's own daily upload limit reached, resumes 22:00", and notified as a
  normal (not alert) message. The whole remote waits until the next window
  starts instead of the rest of its queue failing the same way seconds later.
  Real, unrelated errors still read as errors.
###0.6.1
- Fixed a job that hit its daily upload cap while almost no budget was
  left showing as "error" instead of a calm "paused". When the remaining
  budget is too small for even one file, rclone's cutoff cancels the sync
  mid-directory-listing and reports "context canceled" errors; those are
  now recognised as the cutoff's own side effect, so the run is stored as a
  budget stop and the 20% resume threshold holds the job back instead of
  restarting it on every trickle of freed budget. A --max-duration (window)
  stop is unaffected, and a genuine error with no cancellation text still
  reads as an error.
###0.6.0
- A selective job now spans every share from one job instead of being
  locked to one: the tri-state tree's top level lists all your shares, and
  ticks anywhere become the job's selection — no more creating one job per
  share to pick folders across them. Destination layout:
  remote:godwit/_selective/&lt;job name&gt;/&lt;share&gt;/&lt;path...&gt;. Two selective
  jobs (or a share job and a selective job) whose destinations would
  overlap are refused at save time, in either order.
- The Add job "Share" field is now a dropdown of your real shares instead
  of free text — a typo can no longer save a job that only fails at
  22:00. appdata/system/domains are shown, flagged as live data, never
  hidden.
- A 0.5.0-shaped selective job (one share, share-relative paths) loads and
  migrates automatically — no manual fix-up needed.
###0.5.0
- Phase 4: OneDrive selective backup. A new "selective" job type lets you
  hand-pick individual folders/files from a share via a browsable
  tri-state tree (Add job → Browse…), instead of backing up the whole
  share — compiled to an rclone filter file the same way share jobs
  compile their excludes, with real folder/file names safely escaped
  against rclone's glob syntax. On-demand, cached folder/file size
  estimates (no full-tree walk), shown against the target remote's free
  space. A selected file over OneDrive's 250 GiB per-file limit is
  flagged. The daily version-retention purge now creates its own target
  directory before listing it, so a share/remote with no versions yet no
  longer logs "directory not found" noise on every restart. OneDrive's
  daily upload budget is now unlimited by default (previously silently
  inherited Google Drive's 700 GiB cap) — configurable the same way as
  Google's, per PLAN.md.
###0.4.4
- No functional change. Fixed v0.4.3's own new end-to-end test being
  flaky on a fresh checkout (found by the user running it 5 times after
  installing the release): its scenario relied on directory traversal
  order that isn't guaranteed, so the test sometimes failed to reproduce
  the masking behaviour it exists to prove. Rewritten as a single-directory
  scenario that reproduces reliably (15/15 local repeats).
###0.4.3
- Fixed v0.4.2's budget threshold and calm status wording never actually
  engaging: a run that hit the daily upload cap AND had ordinary file
  errors was still recorded as "error", because rclone's own cutoff
  message always loses to a real per-file error in the same run. Godwit
  now also recognises a cap stop by how close the transferred bytes
  landed to the configured limit, not just by the error text — a real
  error is still shown, just alongside the correct "paused" status, not
  in place of it.
###0.4.2
- Godwit no longer restarts a job that hit its daily upload cap on every
  small trickle of freed budget: it now waits until at least 20% of the
  remote's cap is free again before resuming a budget-stopped job (a job
  that completed cleanly last time is unaffected). A budget or window
  stop now reads as a calm "paused, resumes HH:MM" instead of "error" on
  the Settings page. The page no longer says "Phase 3".
###0.4.1
- Fixed spurious "run failed" alerts on Kieren/Teegan (2026-09-18): a
  MaxTransfer/daily-cap cutoff always left some destination directories the
  cutoff never reached, and rclone's own directory-modtime pass then failed
  on every one of them with "directory not found" — that masking error, not
  a real transfer failure, was what got stored and notified as "run
  failed". Every sync/copy job now sets rclone's NoUpdateDirModTime (no
  value for an offsite backup, and it can never succeed against a
  destination directory that doesn't exist yet), so a cap-stopped run's
  real cutoff message survives uncontaminated.
- A run that stopped at the daily cap or the nightly window now notifies as
  a normal stop ("stopped at the daily cap"/"stopped for tonight's window",
  how much transferred, that it resumes next window) instead of an alert —
  genuine transfer errors occurring in the same run still surface as
  errors (see CHANGELOG.md for the exact rule).
###0.4.0
- Fixed: version retention purge never ran (rcd rejected operations/list
  for missing the required remote param, so old Drive versions were never
  cleaned up). See CHANGELOG.md for detail.
- Settings page UX rework: no functional change to the job engine, budget
  ledger or scheduling semantics. Adding a Google Drive or OneDrive remote,
  and the OneDrive drive picker, are now jQuery UI dialogs instead of
  inline page furniture, with their own persistent success/error message
  areas that stay open on error. The page is grouped into collapsible
  Status/Remotes/Jobs/Windows and speed/Advanced sections (native
  details/summary), with Status and Jobs open by default. The Windows and
  speed section is now a generated form (day-of-week checkboxes, half-hourly
  start/end dropdowns, a speed field) instead of a raw JSON textarea; the
  raw JSON is still available, kept in sync, in the collapsed Advanced
  section as an escape hatch.
###0.3.0
- Phase 3: Google full backup. One job per share (Filing Cabinet, Kieren,
  Teegan, Photos) syncing /mnt/user/&lt;share&gt; to gdrive:godwit/&lt;share&gt;
  with --backup-dir versioning, Kieren's TimeMachine/BombVault excluded. A
  rolling-24h 700 GiB upload budget, a default 22:00-06:00 250 Mbit/s
  window with a "Run now" override, one active job per remote, version
  retention (30 days), and a Jobs section on the Settings page showing
  per-job progress and per-remote budget. This is the first release that
  writes to Google Drive — see CHANGELOG.md for the full detail.
###0.2.4
- Replaced 0.2.3's "cannot choose automatically" OneDrive ambiguity error
  with a real drive picker: when an account has more than one drive, Add
  now offers a radio list (pre-selecting the drive named exactly
  "OneDrive", if any) instead of failing.
- Before calling rcd for an Add or Re-authorise, a pasted token's expiry is
  checked; an already-expired (or about-to-expire) token is refused with a
  clear message instead of a confusing rclone error (an upstream rclone
  bug means it can't refresh mid-setup — see CHANGELOG.md).
###0.2.3
- Fixed a real OneDrive add failing with "Failed to query root for drive
  \"false\"": the config walker answered every rclone wizard question with
  a blanket "false" except the account-type question, so the drive-selection
  question got "false" instead of a real drive ID. It now answers each
  question by name: the drive question is answered from the offered drive
  list (picks the personal one automatically when unambiguous, otherwise
  reports the drive names and asks you to pick), and an unrecognised
  question fails with a clear message instead of guessing.
- The chosen OneDrive drive's name is now shown in the Add success message.
###0.2.2
- Fixed the Remotes page swallowing every Test/Add/Reauth/Delete result:
  the background remotes-list refresh was wiping the message div meant for
  the user's own action milliseconds after it appeared. Messages now
  persist until the next action, buttons show a pending label and disable
  while running, and every request has a failure handler.
- Add now tolerates pasting rclone authorize's whole output (including its
  "Paste the following..."/"End paste" wrapper lines), not just the bare
  JSON. Name fields are pre-filled with real default values instead of
  placeholders that silently submitted as blank.
- A health check that times out is now recorded as an error ("timed out
  after Ns"), not "unchecked" — unchecked is reserved for "never checked".
  operations/about and the OneDrive/Drive config walk get a 60s timeout.
  A transient error now only notifies after 2 consecutive failed checks;
  auth-expired still notifies immediately.
- On-demand checks and add/reauth/delete actions are now logged to
  /var/log/godwit.log, success or failure, so a "nothing happens" report
  leaves a trace. Log lines now strip control characters, so a remote name
  can't forge a fake second log entry.
###0.2.1
- Found live on the host during 0.2.0's own verification (not caught by
  code review, which doesn't run `ps aux`): rcd's generated `--rc-user`/
  `--rc-pass` were on the spawned process's command line, visible to any
  other local process via `ps aux` or its /proc/PID/cmdline. Fixed by
  passing them as `RCLONE_RC_USER`/`RCLONE_RC_PASS` environment variables
  instead — confirmed locally that the bundled rclone v1.75.1 honours
  these identically to the flags. No functional change otherwise.
###0.2.0
- Phase 2: a Remotes section on the Godwit page lists configured rclone
  remotes (name, type, health status, quota) and can add a Google Drive or
  OneDrive remote from a pasted `rclone authorize` token, re-authorise,
  test-connect and delete one — all via rcd's config API, never the bundled
  CLI, so a live rcd always sees the change immediately. No client_secret,
  client_id or token value is ever returned to the browser. godwitd now
  health-checks every configured remote hourly (read-only `operations/about`
  only — no writes to Drive/OneDrive in this phase), stores the result in
  SQLite, detects auth-expiry distinctly from other errors, and sends an
  Unraid notification once per OK/failed transition and once per 90% quota
  crossing.
- Uninstall now preserves &plgPATH; (rclone.conf and any future
  godwit.cfg/jobs.json) instead of deleting it — it holds live OAuth
  credentials as of this release. Only the installed .txz package files are
  removed from that directory.
###0.1.3
- The install block bundled a new .txz on every upgrade into
  /boot/config/plugins/godwit/ but never removed the old one (~20MB each,
  rclone bundled in) — confirmed on the live host holding both the 0.1.1
  and 0.1.2 package files. After upgradepkg succeeds, the install block
  now deletes every old godwit-version.txz in that directory except the
  one just installed, matching only the numeric-version .txz shape so it
  never touches rclone.conf or any other file there. Safe under set -e
  when the glob matches nothing, and no files are deleted if upgradepkg
  itself fails.
###0.1.2
- Fixes a boot-time install failure: the install block runs under `set -e`,
  and array-ready.sh's expected non-zero exit at boot (array not started,
  cache not mounted — the exact scenario the 0.1.1 deferral branch exists
  for) was left as a bare statement, so `set -e` aborted the whole install
  block before it ever reached the deferral branch, reporting the plugin
  install as failed. The result is now captured via an `if`/`else` instead
  of a bare command.
###0.1.1
- Fixes a boot-order bug: plugins install ~14s before the cache pool is
  mounted, so 0.1.0's unconditional `rc.godwit start` in the install step
  could create godwit's state dir on the RAM rootfs, silently covered by
  the later cache mount and lost at shutdown. godwitd now starts via the
  `started`/`stopping_svcs` emhttpd event hooks (matching file.activity,
  unbalanced and tips.and.tweaks), and the install step only starts it
  immediately when the array is already up. godwitd also refuses to run at
  all if `/mnt/cache` isn't a real mountpoint, and `rc.godwit stop`
  escalates to SIGKILL (daemon and its rcd child) if a graceful stop times
  out.
###0.1.0
- Initial scaffold release: plugin installs, registers an empty Settings/Godwit
  page, and runs a supervised daemon (rc.godwit / godwitd) that starts and
  restarts a single bundled rclone rcd process (unix socket, or a
  127.0.0.1 fallback with generated credentials — never --rc-no-auth). No
  remotes, jobs or uploads yet — this release proves the packaging and
  release pipeline plus D10's bundled, SHA256-verified rclone binary.
</CHANGES>

<FILE Name="&plgPATH;/&name;-&version;.txz">
<URL>&gitURL;/&name;-&version;.txz</URL>
<MD5>&md5;</MD5>
</FILE>

<!--
Install step. Every script invoked below is checked with `-f` (file exists),
never `-x` (executable bit) — an executable-bit check can silently skip a
script that lost its bit in transit. See CLAUDE.md non-negotiable 4.
-->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
set -e

# Stop any already-running daemon from a previous version first — rc.godwit's
# start is idempotent (a no-op while a live pid exists), so without this an
# upgrade would silently leave the OLD process (and its rcd child) running
# under the newly unpacked files instead of restarting it.
if [[ -f &emhttp;/scripts/rc.godwit ]]; then
  bash &emhttp;/scripts/rc.godwit stop || true
fi

# Remove any previously installed package version before upgrading — leaves
# no stale package entry in /var/log/packages from an older release.
for pkg in /var/log/packages/&name;-*; do
  if [[ -f "$pkg" ]]; then
    removepkg "$(basename "$pkg")"
  fi
done

upgradepkg --install-new &plgPATH;/&name;-&version;.txz

# Delete superseded package files from flash — only after upgradepkg has
# succeeded. Each .txz bundles rclone (~20MB); leaving every past version
# on the USB flash drive on every upgrade is wasteful and unbounded.
for old in &plgPATH;/&name;-[0-9]*.txz; do
  if [[ -f "$old" ]]; then
    if [[ "$old" != "&plgPATH;/&name;-&version;.txz" ]]; then
      rm -f "$old"
    fi
  fi
done

mkdir -p &plgPATH;

# Plugins install ~14s before the cache pool mounts at boot (confirmed from
# /boot/logs/syslog on the live host) — starting godwitd unconditionally
# here, as 0.1.0 did, can create its state dir on the still-unmounted
# /mnt/cache's RAM rootfs. Only start now if the array is already up (a
# mid-session install/upgrade); a boot-time install defers to the
# `started` event hook, which fires once the array and cache are actually
# ready. See array-ready.sh.
# The install block runs under `set -e`, so array-ready.sh's expected
# non-zero exit (the whole point of this check, on the exact boot-time
# scenario this release exists to handle) must be consumed by an `if`
# condition, never left as a bare statement — a bare statement's failure
# would abort the install script before it ever reaches the "defer to the
# started hook" branch below.
READY=1
if [[ -f &emhttp;/scripts/array-ready.sh ]]; then
  if bash &emhttp;/scripts/array-ready.sh; then
    READY=0
  else
    READY=1
  fi
fi
if [[ -f &emhttp;/scripts/rc.godwit ]]; then
  if [[ "$READY" -eq 0 ]]; then
    bash &emhttp;/scripts/rc.godwit start
  else
    echo "&name;: array not started yet — godwitd will start via the 'started' event hook"
  fi
fi

echo "&name;: install complete"
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<!--
Remove step. Stops the daemon (which stops its rcd child), removes the
package and the installed tree, and any runtime pid/log leftovers. As of
0.2.0, &plgPATH; (rclone.conf and any future godwit.cfg/jobs.json) is
deliberately preserved — it now holds live OAuth credentials for Google
Drive/OneDrive remotes, and losing them on every uninstall/reinstall cycle
would mean re-authorising by hand each time. Only the installed .txz package
files are removed from that directory; everything else there survives. See
README.md for how to delete rclone.conf by hand if a full wipe is wanted.
-->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>
if [[ -f &emhttp;/scripts/rc.godwit ]]; then
  bash &emhttp;/scripts/rc.godwit stop || true
fi

removepkg &name;-&version; || true

rm -rf &emhttp;
rm -f &plgPATH;/&name;-*.txz
rm -rf /var/run/godwit

rm -f /var/run/godwit.pid
rm -f /var/log/godwit.log

echo "&name;: removed (rclone.conf and any other config preserved under &plgPATH;)"
</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
